<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antinoopolis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">130 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Antinoe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antinoopolis was a city founded by the emperor Hadrian in 130 CE on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a>, near the place where his favourite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antinous">Antinous</a> had drowned. Settled with Greek colonists and given special privileges, it later became a capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a>. Its ruins lie near the modern village of Sheikh Ibada.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antinoopolis.jpg" alt="Antinoopolis" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Impr. impériale · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antino%C3%AB_(Antino%C3%B6polis)._Vue_de_l%27arc_de_triomphe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
